  "interpretation_notes": [
    "Non-overlapping vertical aggregation can improve random-noise SNR only to the extent that range-bin errors are not fully correlated.",
    "For this case, lag-1 residual correlation is modest (~0.10-0.13 for the high-altitude PC channels) and longer lags are near zero, so an empirical stationary equal-variance model predicts substantial but sub-sqrt(N) gain.",
    "At 15-20 km, 60-120 m aggregation can raise the median block-level SNR above the native-grid value under an independence-like model, but there is essentially no gain under the fully-correlated uncertainty limit.",
    "At 20-25 km even aggressive aggregation remains weak in this case; smoothing cannot manufacture trustworthy information where the underlying signal is too weak.",
    "This experiment does not authorize productive smoothing, a new SNR threshold, or a higher KFS boundary. Synthetic bias/resolution tests and broader observational evidence are required."
  ]
